Measured while checking the documentation after the 0.500 work, prompted by
#115 -- which was not a stray broken link but one symptom of this.

Public methods carrying an =method / =attr / =head2 block, per module
(execute / BUILD / DEMOLISH / BUILDARGS excluded):

  ActivityLog.pm            5/5    100%
  Error.pm                  3/3    100%
  Role/BoardDiscovery.pm    1/1    100%
  Role/ExitCodes.pm         1/1    100%
  Role/SyncLifecycle.pm     2/2    100%
  Role/TaskMutation.pm      5/5    100%
  SyncGuard.pm              3/3    100%
  Config.pm                19/26    73%
  BoardStore.pm            19/34    55%
  Task.pm                   5/11    45%
  Role/Output.pm            1/2     50%
  Role/BoardAccess.pm       3/12    25%
  Git.pm                    4/52     7%
  Encoding.pm               0/9      0%
  Lock.pm                   0/12     0%
  Foundation.pm             0/1      0%
  Role/ClaimTimeout.pm      0/2      0%
  Role/CliArgs.pm           0/2      0%

This is not a policy of leaving internals undocumented. Git.pm, Encoding.pm,
Lock.pm and ActivityLog.pm are all set up identically -- ABSTRACT, DESCRIPTION
and SYNOPSIS each -- and ActivityLog documents every method while Git.pm
documents four. Whoever reads the dist cannot tell which modules are meant to
be used and which are not, because the presentation says the same thing about
all of them.

Two are worth doing before the rest:

- Git.pm, 4 of 52, and it is the module everything else goes through. #115 was
  exactly this: last_error is called from Cmd/Sync.pm, SyncGuard.pm and
  Role/SyncLifecycle.pm and had only a `#` comment, which only surfaced because
  something linked to it. The same is true of the ref read/write pairs, the CAS
  variants and the retry helper -- the parts a caller most needs a contract for.
- Encoding.pm, 0 of 9. CLAUDE.md makes it the architectural boundary ("nothing
  else may Encode::encode/decode, call YAML::XS::Dump/Load, or
  encode_json/decode_json directly"), so the rule that governs the whole dist
  lives in a file agents read and not in the POD of the module that enforces it.
  from_octets / to_octets / yaml_dump / yaml_load / json_encode / json_decode
  are precisely the surface that rule is about.

Not a release blocker: podchecker is clean across every woven module (that was
#115), every .pm has its ABSTRACT, and nothing here is wrong -- it is missing.
Work for karr-pod-writer, sensibly split per module rather than as one sweep.
Correction 2026-08-11, my error: the table above lists Encoding.pm at 0/9, which is wrong. The scan counted =method / =attr / =head2 and not =func, and Encoding.pm documents all nine functions with =func -- added in 46f006b, together with a DESCRIPTION that reproduces the CLAUDE.md boundary rule and links each function. =func is house-sanctioned: Pod::Elemental::Transformer::Author::GETTY turns =attr, =method and =func into =head2 alike, verified in the woven output. So the claim that the encoding rule lives only in CLAUDE.md and not in the module enforcing it was false.

Re-measured against HEAD with =func included, the modules with real gaps are: Git.pm 4/52, Lock.pm 0/12, Foundation.pm 0/1, Role/ClaimTimeout 0/2, Role/CliArgs 0/2, Role/BoardAccess 3/12, Role/Output 1/2, Task.pm 5/11, BoardStore.pm 19/34, Config.pm 19/26. Everything else was already complete. The rest of this ticket stands.

Encoding.pm was still improved rather than reverted: each of the six codec functions now says locally that it is the only place karr calls the raw Encode / YAML::XS / *_json function, so a reader of to_octets does not have to jump to DESCRIPTION to learn which side of the boundary they are on.
